WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.183
That afternoon Dr. Craven came to see Colin.

2
00:00:06.184 --> 00:00:09.449
He always visited after a temper tantrum.

3
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:16.770
"Last night Mary flew at Colin like a cat," Mrs. Medlock explained as she led the doctor upstairs.

4
00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:21.053
"She stamped her feet and ordered him to stop screaming.

5
00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:25.402
Somehow she startled him so that he stopped.

6
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:30.510
And today . . . well, you won't believe your eyes."

7
00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:36.145
In Colin's room he and Mary were talking and laughing over a book.

8
00:00:36.146 --> 00:00:40.979
But as soon as they noticed the adults, the two children grew silent.

9
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.380
"I'm sorry to hear you're ill, my boy," said Dr. Craven.

10
00:00:45.381 --> 00:00:51.999
"I'm much better now. I'm going out in my wheelchair soon, if the weather is good.

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.719
I want some fresh air."

12
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.504
Dr. Craven sat down next to him. "I thought you didn't like fresh air."

13
00:01:00.505 --> 00:01:04.959
"I don't when I'm by myself, but Mary is coming with me."

14
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.770
"And so is your nurse, of course."

15
00:01:07.771 --> 00:01:14.175
"No, I won't need the nurse." Colin sounded like a rajah again.

16
00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:17.320
"I'm always better when Mary is with me.

17
00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:21.520
A very strong boy I know will push my wheelchair."

18
00:01:21.521 --> 00:01:24.859
Dr. Craven felt alarmed for a moment.

19
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.975
If Colin got well, then the doctor would lose all chance of inheriting Misselthwaite Manor.

20
00:01:30.976 --> 00:01:37.419
But Dr. Craven was not a bad man. He wouldn't let Colin run into danger.

21
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.972
"This boy who's pushing you must be strong," said Dr. Craven.

22
00:01:41.973 --> 00:01:46.054
"And I must know something about him. Who is he?"

23
00:01:46.055 --> 00:01:49.023
"It's Dickon," said Mary.

24
00:01:49.024 --> 00:01:53.361
She was sure that everyone knew Dickon, and she was right.

25
00:01:53.362 --> 00:01:57.296
"Oh, you'll be safe with him," said Dr. Craven.

26
00:01:57.297 --> 00:02:00.681
"He's as strong as a moor pony."

27
00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:06.551
That afternoon Dr. Craven did not give any medicine or leave instructions for the nurse.

28
00:02:06.552 --> 00:02:12.993
"This certainly is a new state of things," he said to Mrs. Medlock as they went downstairs.

29
00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:17.329
That night Colin slept peacefully for the first time.

30
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:23.102
He had been awake for only ten minutes the next morning, when Mary ran into his room.

31
00:02:23.103 --> 00:02:29.240
"There's that nice smell again!" cried Colin. "You've been outside!"

32
00:02:29.241 --> 00:02:36.695
"You never saw anything so beautiful," said Mary. "Spring has really come!"

33
00:02:36.696 --> 00:02:42.686
"Has it?" Colin sat up in bed. "Open the window."

34
00:02:42.687 --> 00:02:48.603
When Mary opened the window, fresh smells and birdsongs came pouring into the room.

35
00:02:48.604 --> 00:02:54.147
"Lie on your back and breathe deeply. That's what Dickon does."

36
00:02:54.148 --> 00:03:04.278
Colin did as he was told. He lay still and breathed in the fresh air while Mary described everything she had seen in the garden that morning.

37
00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:10.019
She also told him how Dickon had rescued a newborn lamb whose mother had died.

38
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.227
After a while the nurse brought breakfast for Colin and Mary.

39
00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:18.763
"When do you think Dickon will come?" asked Colin.

40
00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:27.063
"Soon," replied Mary. And in a few minutes, they heard a crow cawing.

41
00:03:27.064 --> 00:03:33.739
"That's Soot," said Mary. "Listen. Do you hear a tiny sound?"

42
00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.068
"Oh, yes!" cried Colin.

43
00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:40.966
"That's the lamb. Dickon's coming."

44
00:03:40.967 --> 00:03:49.199
In a moment Martha opened the door and announced, "If you please, sir, here's Dickon and his creatures."

45
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.390
Dickon entered the room, wearing his nicest smile.

46
00:03:53.391 --> 00:03:58.755
The newborn lamb was in his arms, and the fox trotted by his side.

47
00:03:58.756 --> 00:04:02.808
Nut sat on one shoulder and Soot on the other.

48
00:04:02.809 --> 00:04:06.082
Shell's head peeped out of his coat pocket.

49
00:04:06.083 --> 00:04:12.341
Colin sat up slowly and stared. He had never seen anyone like this.

50
00:04:12.342 --> 00:04:15.147
He didn't know what to say.

51
00:04:15.148 --> 00:04:19.458
But Colin's behavior did not make Dickon feel shy or awkward.

52
00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.390
Wild creatures always stared at him until they knew him.

53
00:04:23.391 --> 00:04:28.039
He walked over to the sofa and laid the lamb on Colin's lap.

54
00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:32.266
The lamb began to butt its head against Colin's side.

55
00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:35.766
"What is it doing?" asked Colin.

56
00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.763
"It wants its mother.

57
00:04:37.764 --> 00:04:42.179
I brought it to you a bit hungry because I knew you'd like to see it feed."

58
00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.670
Dickon knelt by the sofa and took a bottle out of his pocket.

59
00:04:46.671 --> 00:04:53.923
Colin watched in amazement while Dickon fed the lamb and told them how he'd discovered it on the moor.

60
00:04:53.924 --> 00:04:57.364
"I heard a weak cry, but couldn't find it.

61
00:04:57.365 --> 00:05:01.733
At last I spotted a bit of white near a rock."

62
00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:06.050
As Dickon talked, Soot flew in and out of the window.

63
00:05:06.051 --> 00:05:12.315
Nut and Shell made trips to the big trees outside and ran up and down the branches.

64
00:05:12.316 --> 00:05:16.754
Captain curled up near Dickon after the lamb fell asleep.

65
00:05:16.755 --> 00:05:22.713
The children looked at some of Colin's books, which were full of pictures of flowers.

66
00:05:22.714 --> 00:05:29.268
"We call that one columbine," said Dickon, pointing to a flower that had a long Latin name in the book.

67
00:05:29.269 --> 00:05:32.694
"There are big clumps of columbine in the garden.

68
00:05:32.695 --> 00:05:37.212
They'll look like a bed of blue and white butterflies when they're blooming."

69
00:05:37.213 --> 00:05:40.783
"I'm going to see them," Colin said firmly.

70
00:05:40.784 --> 00:05:50.562
Mary nodded solemnly. "Yes. Yes, you are," she said.

